---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const generatedAt = new Date().toISOString();
---

<Layout
  title="Caching"
  lede="One rule in the config turns into the headers the bunny.net CDN reads."
  badge="routeRules"
>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Generated at</th>
          <td class="value" id="generated-at">{generatedAt}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin: 0.9rem 0 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem">
      On the live site this timestamp holds still for a minute, because the edge answers from its
      cache. Every other page changes on every reload.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The rule</h2>
  <pre><code>{`// astro.config.mjs
routeRules: {
  "/cached": { maxAge: 60, swr: 600, tags: ["demo"] },
}`}</code></pre>

  <h2>The headers it produces</h2>
  <pre><code>Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate, s-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=600
CDN-Tag: demo,astro-path:/cached</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>s-maxage</code> is for the CDN and <code>max-age</code> is for the browser. Splitting them is
    what makes a purge take effect at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Purging</h2>
  <pre><code>{`await Astro.cache.invalidate({ tags: ["demo"] });`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    That calls the bunny.net purge API for the <code>demo</code> tag. Give the script
    <code>BUNNY_API_KEY</code> and <code>BUNNY_PULLZONE_ID</code> first.
  </p>
</Layout>
